@@ -115,7 +115,9 @@ The schemas are hand-maintained per vendor and are not identical in their constr
 
 Detection of that violation is where they diverge. `PetController` recognizes it by matching the constraint name inside the exception message, which couples the controller to a schema identifier. H2 and PostgreSQL name the constraint `unique_owner_pet_name`; MySQL declares it unnamed and is auto-assigned a name derived from its first column, so the match fails and the violation is rethrown rather than converted to a field error. The consequence is recorded under [Known Defects](#known-defects).
 
-Open sessions are not held across view rendering (`spring.jpa.open-in-view` is disabled), so associations that a view needs are fetched eagerly at the mapping level.
+Open sessions are not held across view rendering (`spring.jpa.open-in-view` is disabled), so associations that a view needs are fetched eagerly at the mapping level. A graph a controller reads is therefore detached, and mutating it writes nothing until a repository save.
+
+`Pet.visits` is a `LinkedHashSet` and `BaseEntity` defines no `equals`, so two instances of one visit row occupy two elements. `VisitController` loads the owner, the pet, and the visit under correction from one `OwnerRepository.findById` call. Form binding then mutates the visit inside that graph, and `save` cascades an update to the existing row. `PetController` resolves its owner and its pet through separate lookups, so it copies the bound values onto the stored pet instead.
 
